.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAG 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eag \- publish paths through the API gateway onto euclid applications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B eag
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B eag
module configures euclid's API gateway: an HTTP listener of its own that
publishes paths to the outside world and proxies each request to one of
the running instances of a
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
application.
.PP
It exists because an autoscaled application has no fixed address. Eight
instances of a parser are eight processes on eight ports, and those
ports change as the autoscaler starts and stops them \- so nothing
outside can be told where to send a request. The gateway is the fixed
address: one port that does not move, in front of a set of backends that
does.
.PP
This is not the same listener as
.I euclid.gateway.http.port
(5566), which carries euclid's own service calls \- the ones every other
module in this manual is reached through. That one speaks euclid's
protocol and routes by
.IR x-euclid-target .
The API gateway speaks nothing but HTTP and routes by path, because what
is behind it is an ordinary web application that knows nothing about
euclid.
.SH SEVERAL ENVIRONMENTS, SEVERAL PORTS
One gateway can answer on more than one port, each bound to a namespace:
.PP
.RS
.nf
"eag": {
  "listeners": {
    "development": { "port": 8080 },
    "integration": { "port": 8081 }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A request is then in the namespace of the port it arrived on, and a
listener carries only that namespace's routes. Development and
integration can both publish
.I /api/produktmeldungen
without either being ambiguous, because the port says which was meant.
.PP
The alternative \- one port and the environment in the path,
.I /api/development/produktmeldungen
\- puts euclid's own structure into addresses that outlive it, and makes
the application see a segment that has nothing to do with it, since the
path is forwarded unchanged.
.PP
Routes naming no namespace are carried by every listener, so an
installation that never configures one keeps working: a bare
.I port
means a single listener that serves everything, which is what a
production installation \- usually its own euclid, with one account and
one namespace \- actually wants.
.PP
What this does not solve is application naming. The manager keys its
pools by
.IR applicationId ,
which is unique across the installation, so two environments on one
euclid need
.I parser-dev
and
.I parser-int
rather than two applications called
.IR parser .
.SH ROUTES
A route is a path prefix plus the application it belongs to. Everything
beneath the prefix is carried, and the path is forwarded
.I unchanged
\- a route on
.I /resource
pointing at
.I parser
sends
.PP
.RS
.nf
GET /resource/searchById?id=123
.fi
.RE
.PP
to the parser exactly as written. The application sees the URL its
callers typed; nothing about the route, the gateway or the application's
own name appears in it. An application therefore does not have to know
it is behind a gateway, and moving a path from one application to
another changes no URL.
.PP
Longest prefix wins. A route on
.I /resource
carries everything below it, and a more specific
.I /resource/export
added later takes precedence for its own subtree without either being
reordered \- the more specific one is simply longer.
.PP
A prefix only matches on a segment boundary, so
.I /resource
never claims
.IR /resource\-intern ,
which is a different resource that happens to start with the same
letters.
.PP
.SH METHODS
A route may name the HTTP methods it answers for. Naming none \- the
default \- means all of them, so a route written today does not quietly
refuse a method that did not exist when it was written.
.PP
Two routes may share a path as long as their methods do not overlap.
That is what lets one resource be served by two applications, reads by
one and writes by another, with no seam a caller can see:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route -r produkte-lesen  -p /api/produkte \e
    -a parser --methods GET,HEAD
euclid-cli eag create-route -r produkte-pflege -p /api/produkte \e
    -a pim-writer --methods POST,PUT,DELETE
.fi
.RE
.PP
An overlap is refused, because the winner would be decided by whichever
route the gateway happened to consider first \- callers would reach one
application today and possibly the other tomorrow. A route naming no
methods overlaps with everything, so it cannot share a path at all.
.PP
Matching takes the method into account rather than filtering on it
afterwards: a
.B GET
that a POST-only route would reject falls through to the route that does
want it. Only when no route on the path accepts the method is the
request refused, with
.B 405
and an
.I Allow
header listing what the routes on that path do accept.
.SH REACHING EUCLID ITSELF
A route usually names an application. It can instead name a euclid
module and one of its actions, which is how something outside euclid
reaches euclid:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route -r login -p /auth/login \e
    --module eam --action login --methods POST
.fi
.RE
.PP
Credentials go in a JSON body, and the answer carries both kinds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
curl \-X POST http://localhost:8080/auth/login \e
     \-H 'Content\-Type: application/json' \e
     \-d '{"userId":"jvo","password":"secret"}'

{ "token": "<jwt>",          # bearer, valid one hour
  "accessKeyId": "AKIA...",  # for RFC 9421 and SigV4
  "secretAccessKey": "...",
  "createdAt": "..." }
.fi
.RE
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
does exactly this. Without a route like it, a browser application would
talk to the API gateway for the application and to euclid's own gateway
for its credentials \- two ports, two origins, and CORS between them.
.PP
.B Not query parameters.
A password in a query string is written to access logs, sent onward in
.I Referer
headers and kept in browser history; in a body it is none of those
things. Giving the route
.B \-\-methods POST
is what makes that stick, since a
.B GET
carrying credentials in its URL is then answered with 405 rather than
being forwarded.
.PP
The login body crosses the wire as it is written, so a gateway reachable
from anywhere but this host wants TLS in front of it.
.I euclid.gateway.tls.*
covers euclid's own gateway on 5566, not the API gateway's port \- see
.B LIMITATIONS
below.
.PP
The request is forwarded to euclid's own gateway rather than to the
module. A module listens on a Unix domain socket and has no address
anything outside the host could reach; the gateway is what turns a
target and an action into a call on one, and is already what every SDK
talks to.
.PP
.B One action per route, deliberately.
A route that took its action from the rest of the path would publish
every action that module has \- including the ones that delete users \-
and publishing an administrative interface by accident is not a mistake
that announces itself. Naming the action means what is exposed is
exactly what somebody wrote down.
.PP
A login route needs
.B \-\-authentication none
for the obvious reason: it is where a credential comes from. Everything
else a module route publishes should almost certainly require one.
.PP
The account and namespace headers a caller sends are passed through
untouched. That is safe because the module checks them against what the
caller is actually granted, so naming somebody else's account is refused
rather than obeyed.
.PP
.I x\-euclid\-region
and
.I x\-euclid\-namespace
are supplied from the route when the caller does not send them. Euclid
refuses a request naming no region once
.I euclid.region
is configured \- correct between modules, where every client sends it,
and not something a browser could know to do.
.PP
From the route rather than from configuration, because a namespace is a
property of what is published and not of the installation: two routes on
one gateway may belong to different namespaces, and only the route says
which. They are set when the route is created, defaulting to whoever
created it, and can be named explicitly \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.PP
Only when the caller sends neither. A client that names a region or a
namespace keeps what it named and is refused if it is not permitted,
which is a better answer than being moved somewhere else without being
told.
.SH BACKENDS
The instances behind a route are not configured. The manager gives every
application instance a TCP port of its own out of the range set by
.I euclid.modules.eap.http\-port\-min
and
.I euclid.modules.eap.http\-port\-max
(9000\-9999 by default) and records it, and the gateway reads that record
\- so an application that scales from one instance to eight is spread
across eight backends within a few seconds, and one that scales back
down stops being sent to, without any route being touched.
.PP
Requests go to the instances in turn, one after another. An instance the
manager could not give a port to is left out of the rotation rather than
being sent to and failing.
.PP
An application must set its own listener to the port it is given. For
Spring Boot that is
.PP
.RS
.nf
server:
  port: ${EUCLID_HTTP_PORT:8080}
.fi
.RE
.PP
which is what makes several instances able to run on one host at all:
without it every instance binds the same port, the first one wins, and
the rest fail in a way the autoscaler cannot see.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Each route decides what it requires of a caller, because the two kinds of
resource sit behind the same listener.
.TP
.B NONE
Proxied as it arrives. Whatever the application requires, it enforces
itself. This is what a route behind an external API gateway wants, and
what a public read wants.
.TP
.B EUCLID
A euclid credential is required and verified before anything is
forwarded \- whatever the euclid gateway accepts, which today is a
bearer token from
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
an RFC 9421 message signature, or SigV4. The check happens before a
backend is chosen, so an unauthenticated caller never causes a
connection to an application at all.
.TP
.B BASIC
HTTP Basic, checked against the same euclid user passwords. An
unauthenticated caller is answered with
.PP
.RS
.RS
.nf
WWW\-Authenticate: Basic realm="euclid", charset="UTF\-8"
.fi
.RE
.PP
which is the whole point of it: a browser shows a username and password
prompt and tries again, where a bearer token would have needed something
to obtain it first. Reach for this when the caller is a person at a
browser, or a script with nothing but
.BR curl (1).
.PP
A technical principal \- an application's identity, with an access key
and
.I loginEnabled
false \- is refused, as it is at login. Those sign with RFC 9421 and have
no password to offer.
.PP
Basic sends the password on every request, base64\-encoded, which is not
encryption. Over plain HTTP it is readable by anything on the path \- see
.B LIMITATIONS.
.RE
.PP
Either way the caller needs to know nothing about euclid's own headers:
.I x\-euclid\-region
and
.I x\-euclid\-namespace
are filled in from the route. See
.B REACHING EUCLID ITSELF
above.
.SH ANSWERS
Five of the gateway's own answers are worth telling apart, since none of
them come from the application:
.TP
.B 404
No route claims this path, or the route that did has been deleted or
deactivated.
.TP
.B 405
A route claims the path but none on it accepts this method. The
.I Allow
header says which methods would have worked.
.TP
.B 401
The route requires a euclid credential and the request had none, or one
that did not verify.
.TP
.B 503
The route names an application that has no running instance \- scaled
to zero, still starting, or given no port. The resource exists and
trying again is reasonable, which is why this is not a 404.
.TP
.B 502
An instance was chosen and did not answer: the connection was refused,
or it took longer than 60 seconds.
.SH CHANGES
The gateway re-reads its routes every
.I euclid.modules.eag.refresh\-seconds
(5 by default), so a route created, changed or deleted here takes effect
shortly after the command returns rather than at the next restart. A
database it cannot read leaves the previous routes in place, so a
momentary outage there does not become an outage of every application
behind the gateway.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-route
Publishes a path prefix and points it at an application. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.TP
.B delete-route
Removes a route, taking its path out of service. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-delete-route (1).
.TP
.B get-route
Shows one route's definition. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-get-route (1).
.TP
.B list-routes
Lists the configured routes. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-list-routes (1).
.TP
.B update-route
Changes an existing route's path, application, authentication or state.
See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-update-route (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I eag
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
Note that this is euclid's own service endpoint, not the API gateway
port these commands configure.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty " " \fIBOOL\fR
Pretty-print the JSON response. Defaults to \fItrue\fR.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme used for signed service calls: \fIrfc9421\fR (the
default) or \fIsigv4\fR for AWS Signature Version 4.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Every
.B eag
action is administrator-only, as in
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1):
a route decides what the installation exposes to the outside world and
whether reaching it needs a credential at all.
.PP
The session is created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
and loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
The CLI checks for it before sending, but that is only a fail-fast for a
clearer message \- the server re-checks every request regardless.
.PP
Requesting help for an action does not require a session, so
.B "euclid-cli eag <action> \-\-help"
works before logging in.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Read from
.I euclid.modules.eag
in
.IR euclid.json :
.TP
.I port
TCP port the API gateway listens on. Defaults to 8080. Ignored when
.I listeners
is given.
.TP
.I listeners
One port per namespace, keyed by namespace \-
.RI { "development" : { "port" : 8080 }} \-
for an installation serving more than one environment. See
.B SEVERAL ENVIRONMENTS, SEVERAL PORTS
above.
.TP
.I proxy\-threads
Threads serving proxied requests. Defaults to 8.
.TP
.I refresh\-seconds
How often routes and backends are re-read from the database. Defaults
to 5.
.TP
.I basic\-auth\-cache\-seconds
How long a verified Basic credential stays verified. Defaults to 60.
Set to 0 to check the password on every request, which is correct and
costs tens of milliseconds of CPU each time \- see
.B BASIC AUTHENTICATION COSTS
below.
.TP
.I maxInstances
Must be 1. Two instances cannot bind the same port, and the autoscaler
cannot see that failure \- it would only see a pool that will not grow.
.SH BASIC AUTHENTICATION COSTS
A euclid password is stored as PBKDF2\-SHA256 at 100,000 iterations. That
is deliberate and right for a login that happens once; it also means
tens of milliseconds of CPU every time a password is checked.
.PP
This is why only the gateway accepts Basic and no euclid module does. A
bearer token or a signature costs an HMAC and nothing more, so the
modules' own paths stay cheap, and the expensive check happens once at
the edge.
.PP
Even there it is not paid per request. A verified credential is
remembered for
.I basic\-auth\-cache\-seconds
(60 by default), so a browser making a hundred calls hashes once and
compares ninety\-nine times. What is kept is a hash of the credential,
never the password, and entries are dropped as they expire.
.PP
Two things follow from the window:
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
a password change, or a user being deleted, takes effect within it
rather than at once. Set it to 0 where that matters more than the CPU;
.IP \(bu 3
without a cache, an unauthenticated caller can spend a core of the
gateway per request. The cache does not help there \- a wrong password is
never cached \- so a route open to the internet wants a rate limit in
front of it, which euclid does not provide.
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The API gateway speaks plain HTTP. There is no
.I euclid.modules.eag.tls
to match
.I euclid.gateway.tls.*
\- an installation exposing this port beyond its own host is expected to
terminate TLS ahead of it. That matters most for a route published with
.B \-\-authentication basic
or one carrying a login, since both put a password in the request.
.PP
Request and response bodies are buffered whole rather than streamed,
which suits the short REST calls the gateway carries today and not a
large download. One request is served per connection; there is no
keep-alive to the caller and no connection pooling to the backends.
.PP
A backend that refuses the connection produces a 502 rather than a retry
against another instance of the same application.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, there is no session or it is not an
administrator's, the request could not be sent, or the server rejected
it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or access key,
created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B eag
action.
.SH EXAMPLES
Publish a parser's REST interface, requiring no credential because an
external gateway in front of euclid authenticates the caller:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id resource \e
    --path /resource \e
    --application onix-parser

curl http://localhost:8080/resource/searchById?id=123
.fi
.RE
.PP
Carve out an administrative subtree of the same application that does
require one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id resource-admin \e
    --path /resource/admin \e
    --application onix-parser \e
    --authentication euclid
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take a path out of service without losing how it was configured, then
put it back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --active false
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --active true
.fi
.RE
